---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import AffordabilityTool from '../../components/AffordabilityTool.astro';
import RelatedTools from '@kit/components/RelatedTools.astro';
import { SITE, toolBySlug, toolsExcept } from '../../lib/site';
import { METROS } from '@data/metros';

const tool = toolBySlug('home-affordability-calculator')!;
const canonical = `/tools/${tool.slug}`;
const title = `Home Affordability Calculator — How Much House Can I Afford | ${SITE.name}`;
const description =
  'Work out the home price your income actually supports, using both DTI ' +
  'limits lenders apply, plus local property tax, insurance and PMI.';

const schema = [{
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE.url },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Calculators', item: `${SITE.url}/tools` },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: tool.nav, item: `${SITE.url}${canonical}` },
  ],
}];

const related = toolsExcept(tool.slug).slice(0, 3).map((t) => ({
  href: `/tools/${t.slug}`, title: t.title, desc: t.blurb,
}));
---
<BaseLayout {title} {description} {canonical} {schema}>
  <AffordabilityTool
    metro={null}
    title="Home Affordability Calculator"
    intro="Lenders apply two debt-to-income limits, and the lower one decides your answer. This solves for the price that fits both — with property tax, insurance and PMI included, not bolted on afterwards."
    breadcrumbs={[{ href: '/', label: 'Home' }, { href: '/tools', label: 'Calculators' }, { label: tool.nav }]}
  >
    <section style="margin-top:var(--s-7)">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2>Affordability by city</h2>
        <p>Property tax rates and insurance premiums vary more between metros than mortgage rates vary between lenders. Start from local figures.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="chip-row">
        {METROS.map((m) => <a class="chip" href={`/affordability/${m.slug}`}>{m.name}, {m.stateCode}</a>)}
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="prose" style="margin-top:var(--s-7)">
      <h2>The two ratios that decide it</h2>
      <p>
        Underwriting comes down to two debt-to-income ratios, and the answer is
        whichever one runs out first.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Front-end DTI</strong> is housing alone as a share of gross monthly
          income — principal, interest, taxes, insurance, PMI and HOA. The
          conventional guideline is 28%.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Back-end DTI</strong> is all recurring debt, housing included: car
          payments, student loans, minimum card payments, child support. The common
          ceiling is 43%, which is also the threshold for a Qualified Mortgage.
          Fannie Mae will go to 50% with compensating factors.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Knowing which one binds tells you what to do next. If the front-end ratio is
        the constraint, more income or a bigger down payment is the only lever. If
        the back-end ratio binds, clearing a monthly obligation raises your price
        immediately and often by more than saving the same amount would.
      </p>
      <div class="callout">
        <strong>What a lender approves is not what you should spend.</strong> These
        ratios use gross income, before tax, retirement contributions, health
        premiums or childcare. A 43% back-end ratio on gross can be 60% or more of
        what actually reaches your account. The maximum is a ceiling, not a target.
      </div>

      <h2>What goes into the payment</h2>
      <h3>Principal and interest</h3>
      <p>
        The only part that pays down the loan, and the only part fixed for the life
        of a fixed-rate mortgage. Everything else can rise.
      </p>
      <h3>Property tax</h3>
      <p>
        Charged as a percentage of assessed value, and it varies more than any other
        input on this page — from roughly 0.3% in Hawaii to over 2.2% in New Jersey.
        On a $400,000 home that is a difference of about $630 a month, which is
        equivalent to more than a full percentage point of mortgage rate. Use the
        local figure, not a national average.
      </p>
      <h3>Homeowners insurance</h3>
      <p>
        Also intensely local. Coastal Florida, tornado-belt Oklahoma and wildfire
        exposure in parts of California and Colorado carry premiums several times
        the national median. This has become the fastest-moving line in the payment
        and is worth quoting before you make an offer, not after.
      </p>
      <h3>PMI</h3>
      <p>
        Required on conventional loans below 20% down, typically 0.3% to 1.5% of the
        loan annually depending on credit score and down payment. It is not
        permanent: it cancels automatically at 78% loan-to-value on the original
        schedule, and you can request removal at 80%. FHA loans are different — most
        carry mortgage insurance for the full term, which is why refinancing out of
        an FHA loan after building equity is so often worth doing.
      </p>

      <h2>What lenders count that people forget</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Student loans in deferment.</strong> Still counted, usually at 0.5–1% of the balance per month even if you pay nothing today.</li>
        <li><strong>Co-signed debt.</strong> If your name is on it, it is your debt, regardless of who actually pays.</li>
        <li><strong>Lease payments.</strong> Counted for the full term, and unlike a loan they never amortize away.</li>
        <li><strong>Variable income.</strong> Bonus, commission and self-employment income generally needs a two-year history and is averaged. A great year on its own will not count.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        What is not counted: utilities, groceries, insurance premiums paid outside
        escrow, childcare, and anything on a card you pay off monthly. Underwriting
        looks at obligations on your credit report, which is both narrower and less
        forgiving than a real budget.
      </p>

      <h2>Common questions</h2>
      <dl class="faq">
        <dt>Should I borrow the maximum?</dt>
        <dd>
          Rarely. The ratios ignore everything that is not a reported debt, and they
          leave no room for the maintenance a house generates. A useful sanity check
          is to live on the new payment for three months before committing —
          transfer the difference between your current housing cost and the proposed
          one into savings and see whether it holds.
        </dd>
        <dt>How much does a rate change move my price?</dt>
        <dd>
          Roughly 10% of purchase price per percentage point at current levels. Drag
          the rate slider and watch the headline — it moves further than most people
          expect, which is why pre-approvals expire.
        </dd>
        <dt>Does a bigger down payment always help?</dt>
        <dd>
          Up to 20%, substantially — it removes PMI and lowers the loan. Past 20% the
          benefit is just the smaller balance, and the cash may be worth more as
          reserves. Lenders like to see two to six months of payments left over after
          closing, and buyers who empty their savings to reach 25% down often find
          the first repair unaffordable.
        </dd>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <RelatedTools items={related} />
  </AffordabilityTool>
</BaseLayout>

<script>
  import '../../scripts/af-island';
</script>
